  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a specific, localized educational collaboration in the Netherlands, which may limit generalizability to other institutional or cultural contexts.",
      "what": "This study analyzes a participatory game development project in the Netherlands where vocational students aged 15 to 28 collaborated with people with disabilities. The partnership produced digital games that addressed spatial and social barriers, mainstreamed disability in narratives, and created accessible gameplay. The research examines how the institutional arrangements, labor distribution, and risk structures of the classroom setting shaped these interactions.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that while structured pedagogical settings can temporarily reconfigure power dynamics and foster social connections between disabled and non-disabled individuals, these organized encounters face significant limitations in shifting broader, everyday geographies outside the classroom."
